[{"short_id":"7ibjyl","created_at":"2026-07-31T06:15:54.655-05:00","title":"Go 1.27 interactive tour","url":"https://victoriametrics.com/blog/go-1-27/","score":25,"flags":0,"comment_count":9,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"cgrinds","user_is_author":false,"tags":["go"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/7ibjyl","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/7ibjyl/go_1_27_interactive_tour"},{"short_id":"ycsivx","created_at":"2026-07-31T00:46:14.699-05:00","title":"How to speed up the Rust compiler in July 2026","url":"https://nnethercote.github.io/2026/07/31/how-to-speed-up-the-rust-compiler-in-july-2026.html","score":52,"flags":0,"comment_count":4,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"asb","user_is_author":false,"tags":["compilers","performance","rust"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ycsivx","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ycsivx/how_speed_up_rust_compiler_july_2026"},{"short_id":"ri8bav","created_at":"2026-07-31T05:36:22.087-05:00","title":"Stripe Just Wants a Number","url":"https://blog.exe.dev/billable-facts","score":16,"flags":0,"comment_count":8,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"mtlynch","user_is_author":false,"tags":["programming"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ri8bav","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ri8bav/stripe_just_wants_number"},{"short_id":"itp1nm","created_at":"2026-07-31T00:05:46.249-05:00","title":"A question about a factorial operator","url":"","score":19,"flags":0,"comment_count":14,"description":"\u003cp\u003eI wrote an assembler that supports floating point values.  One of the operators I implemented was factorial because a lot of Taylor series seem to use it to define various constants.  So right now, I have\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003e.float\t-((2*3.14159265)**11)/11!\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI found a bug in the parsing such that \u003ccode\u003e(2+3)!\u003c/code\u003e doesn't parse (the parsing of the factorial operator is in the wrong location).  In fixing the bug, I found an issue that I don't know how best to resolve, since I personally have never used a programming language that defined a factorial operator.  So my question:  what should \u003ccode\u003e-3!\u003c/code\u003e produce?  Should it error out as factorial isn't defined for negative numbers?  Or should it parse as \u003ccode\u003e-(3!)\u003c/code\u003e and return -6?  I ask because of \u003ccode\u003e-(2+3)!\u003c/code\u003e.  What binds tighter?  The minus, or the factorial?\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"I wrote an assembler that supports floating point values.  One of the operators I implemented was factorial because a lot of Taylor series seem to use it to define various constants.  So right now, I have\r\n\r\n    .float\t-((2*3.14159265)**11)/11!\r\n\r\nI found a bug in the parsing such that `(2+3)!` doesn't parse (the parsing of the factorial operator is in the wrong location).  In fixing the bug, I found an issue that I don't know how best to resolve, since I personally have never used a programming language that defined a factorial operator.  So my question:  what should `-3!` produce?  Should it error out as factorial isn't defined for negative numbers?  Or should it parse as `-(3!)` and return -6?  I ask because of `-(2+3)!`.  What binds tighter?  The minus, or the factorial?","submitter_user":"spc476","user_is_author":true,"tags":["ask","compilers"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/itp1nm","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/itp1nm/question_about_factorial_operator"},{"short_id":"91tzqg","created_at":"2026-07-31T04:37:01.162-05:00","title":"Full flattening of nested data parallelism","url":"https://futhark-lang.org/blog/2026-07-31-full-flattening.html","score":11,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"athas","user_is_author":true,"tags":["programming"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/91tzqg","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/91tzqg/full_flattening_n